---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const sourcesCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Constitutions: the frame', note: 'The supreme source — everything must comply. Amendments are possible but deliberately hard; courts test all other law against it.' },
    { math: '2 · Statutes: what legislatures write', note: 'Written law passed by elected bodies — the primary source of new rules. Interpreted by courts using canons (plain meaning, legislative intent).' },
    { math: '3 · Regulations: the detail layer', note: 'Agencies fill in what statutes leave vague (food safety rules, emission limits). Valid only within the authority the statute granted — ultra vires acts fail.' },
    { math: '4 · Precedent and custom', note: 'Court decisions bind later courts (stare decisis); long-standing practice fills gaps (customary commercial law). The hierarchy resolves conflicts: constitution beats statute beats regulation beats everything else.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/law/intermediate/sources-of-law">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    "The law" is not one book — it is a <strong>hierarchy of sources with a strict chain of
    command</strong>: constitutions above, then statutes, then regulations, then judicial
    precedent, then custom. Every legal argument begins by locating its authority in this
    hierarchy, because the resolution of any conflict depends on which source outranks which.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four rungs and the supremacy rule that orders them" widget="equationStep" config={sourcesCfg} />

  <h2>How courts read the texts</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Plain meaning:</strong> start with what the words ordinarily say — the most common modern starting point.</li>
    <li><strong>Legislative intent and purpose:</strong> what was the rule for? Committees, debates and preambles serve as evidence.</li>
    <li><strong>Canons of construction:</strong> accumulated rules of thumb ("expressio unius": expressing one thing excludes others) — the field's grammar.</li>
    <li><strong>Conflict resolution in practice:</strong> a regulation contradicting a statute fails; a statute contradicting the constitution fails. Most litigation is the search for which level governs.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Glanville Williams, Learning the Law; standard administrative law texts; the "What Makes a Law?" lesson covers precedent in depth.</p>
</TopicPage>
